    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Clausewitz teaches that ending a war is often harder than beginning one. At the outset, political aims are clear and public enthusiasm is high. As conflict drags on, objectives blur, losses accumulate, and expectations shift. The strategist must therefore understand that war termination is a political, psychological, and structural process—not merely a military one. Clausewitz argues that wars end when one side believes further resistance is costlier than settlement. Victory does not always require total collapse of the enemy; it requires convincing them that continuing the struggle is futile. This shift occurs gradually through attrition, fatigue, diplomatic pressure, economic stress, internal dissent, or psychological shock. He warns that leaders often fail because they cling to initial objectives even after conditions have changed. A rational exit demands reassessment: What remains achievable? What costs are acceptable? What can be traded? Clausewitz highlights that refusing to adjust aims leads to prolonged stalemate or catastrophic overextension. War termination also depends on managing domestic expectations. Public opinion is volatile; early enthusiasm can turn into disillusionment. Clausewitz explains that political leaders must maintain legitimacy by aligning public narratives with strategic realities. This requires honesty without panic, firmness without rigidity. Diplomatically, endings are fragile. A victorious side may overreach in negotiations, provoking renewed conflict. The defeated may accept terms only temporarily. Clausewitz insists that settlement must reflect the true balance of power—not illusions, pride, or fear. Durable peace emerges when both sides perceive the agreement as tolerable. He also describes the danger of “victory disease”—when success leads to inflated goals. The strategist must resist temptation to pursue unnecessary gains after achieving the political aim. In modern life, war termination corresponds to deciding when to quit a failing project, scale down a business initiative, end a negotiation, or exit a toxic relationship. The same logic applies: understand sunk costs, adjust aims, preserve dignity, and prevent escalation. Clausewitz’s wisdom: ending well is a strategic victory in itself. The exit is part of the plan, not an afterthought.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/clausewitz_vol2_3_en.mp3"
