    -d '{"text": "Cyrus the Great mastered a form of authority deeper than political power: spiritual legitimacy. He understood that every civilization lives inside a sacred order, a worldview that explains why things are the way they are. Rulers who align themselves with this sacred order gain a form of legitimacy that armies cannot destroy. First, Cyrus respected the religious diversity of his empire. Instead of imposing a single imperial cult, he protected temples, priesthoods, and rituals across regions. This respect signaled that the king honored the cosmic order cherished by each community. Second, he connected his authority to divine favor without claiming divinity. He was portrayed as chosen, guided, or blessed, but not as a god. This balance made his reign sacred without provoking theological rebellion. Third, Cyrus used spiritual legitimacy to unify people emotionally. When Jews saw him as a liberator foretold in scripture, and Babylonians saw him as restorer of Marduk’s order, Cyrus became a figure of universal reassurance. Emotional unity is the most durable unity. Fourth, he integrated priests and scholars into governance. Their counsel shaped policy, and their endorsement shaped public trust. When religious authorities support the state, rebellion feels like a violation of cosmic order. Fifth, spiritual legitimacy strengthened diplomacy. When neighboring regions believed Cyrus upheld divine justice, alliances formed more easily. Sixth, he used sacred storytelling to anchor identity. Inscriptions and decrees framed the empire as a guardian of harmony, not a machine of conquest. People follow a story more willingly than a sword. Seventh, he recognized that sacred legitimacy is fragile. Rulers lose divine favor when they act unjustly. Thus Cyrus tied morality to cosmology: injustice disrupts the cosmic order, while justice restores it. Eighth, Cyrus understood that spiritual legitimacy can survive political change. Even after the fall of Babylonian kings,