    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Cyrus the Great introduced a revolutionary concept in imperial strategy: conquest built on mercy, legitimacy, and liberation rather than annihilation. While ancient empires often relied on terror, enslavement, and scorched-earth tactics, Cyrus recognized that brutality destroys resources, breeds long-term rebellion, and wastes human capital. His approach transformed the psychology of empire. First, Cyrus reframed conquest as liberation. When he captured Babylon, he presented himself not as a destroyer but as a restorer of order, ending abuses by the previous regime. This narrative was more than propaganda; it became a strategic framework that stabilized newly acquired regions without massive garrison forces. By positioning himself as a just ruler rather than a foreign oppressor, he neutralized resistance before it could crystallize. Second, Cyrus practiced targeted leniency. Rather than punishing entire populations, he focused consequences on hostile elites while protecting civilians, artisans, priests, and merchants. This selective mercy preserved economic systems and minimized resentment. Populations that expected ruin instead experienced stability, reducing the need for continuous military oversight. Third, he respected local religions and customs. Unlike conquerors who imposed their own gods, Cyrus restored temples, supported local priesthoods, and allowed displaced peoples such as the Jews to return home. Respect for belief systems became a binding agent that generated deep voluntary loyalty toward the Persian crown. Fourth, Cyrus integrated local leaders into administrative structures. Instead of ruling solely through Persians, he incorporated diverse ethnic groups into governance. This inclusion produced a sense of shared ownership over imperial destiny. Fifth, Cyrus used symbolic gestures to project fairness. His decrees emphasized justice, freedom from oppression, and ethical kingship. These gestures, though symbolic, created a psychological contract between ruler and ruled. The ultimate outcome of humane conquest was durability. Territories taken by Cyrus rarely revolted; they prospered, paid taxes willingly, and participated in imperial systems with minimal coercion. Strategic mercy, in his philosophy, was not softness but a long-range investment in stability. By winning hearts while holding power firmly, Cyrus turned conquest into cooperation and empire into civilization. His example challenges modern strategists to reconsider the rea...", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
