    -d '{"text": "Cyrus the Great understood legitimacy not as a philosophical concept but as a strategic weapon. In his worldview, a ruler who is seen as rightful commands far more stability than a ruler who governs only by fear. Legitimacy reduces resistance, lowers administrative costs, and binds diverse peoples into a coherent imperial identity. Cyrus built legitimacy through a multidimensional strategy. First, he aligned himself with existing traditions. When entering new territories, he respected local gods, legal customs, and ceremonial norms. By doing this, he signaled continuity rather than disruption. Second, he cultivated moral authority. Cyrus portrayed himself as a protector of the weak, restorer of justice, and liberator of oppressed communities. These moral claims were not empty rhetoric; his policies supported them. Moral authority became a form of soft coercion, convincing people to obey not because they must, but because they feel the ruler is just. Third, Cyrus used symbolic acts to strengthen legitimacy. The Cyrus Cylinder, often considered the first known charter of human rights, served as a public manifestation of ethical kingship. Even centuries later, it became evidence of a ruler who practiced principled governance. Fourth, he displayed moderation in power. By limiting unnecessary violence and allowing local autonomy, Cyrus differentiated himself from typical conquerors whose rule was sustained through fear. This moderation created psychological loyalty. Fifth, Cyrus integrated elites from conquered regions into the Persian administrative system. Rather than excluding them, he empowered them, creating a shared political destiny. This reduced internal rebellion and tied influential local leaders to the empire’s survival. Finally, Cyrus understood that legitimacy is fragile. He maintained communication with subjects, listened to grievances, and punished abusive governors. His legitimacy came not from divine claims alone but from consistent performance and fairness. In strategic terms, legitimacy was Cyrus’s invisible fortress — a form of protection stronger than walls or armies. Modern strategists can learn from him that durable power arises not just from force but from earning the right to govern.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
