    -d '{"text": "Epaminondas’ death at Mantinea is one of history’s clearest demonstrations of how deeply transformation can depend on a single individual—and how fragile even the most brilliant reforms become without institutional continuity. First, his strategic system was centralized in his mind rather than embedded in a bureaucracy or school of successors. He was a philosopher-general whose methods were rooted in reasoning, imagination, and a personal ethic that could not be easily reproduced. When he died, Thebes had no second Epaminondas waiting. Second, his reforms were young. The unification of Boeotia, the liberation of Messenia, the creation of Arcadian structures, and the challenge to Spartan hegemony were all less than a decade old. Institutions require time to harden; his had not yet reached durability. Third, his political position in Thebes was never entirely secure. Factions resented his influence, rivals disliked his moral authority, and some elites feared the power he exercised during wartime. This meant that his system lacked a broad coalition ready to carry it forward once he fell. Fourth, his strategic vision required a rare combination of military genius, diplomatic sensibility, philosophical coherence, and personal integrity. Most statesmen possess one or two of these qualities; Epaminondas combined all. After his death, no one could synchronize them. Fifth, his final act of leadership revealed the essence of his character. Mortally wounded, he ordered the retreat not out of cowardice but because he understood that the survival of the army mattered more than symbolic victory. He asked about two things: the condition of the Theban shield-wall and the future of Greece if he died. When told that his spear was still holding the line and that the commanders he trusted remained alive, he allowed the spear that kept his wound from bleeding out to be removed. Sixth, his death halted Theban momentum. Within a few years, Thebes dissipated its gains, Bo