    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Frederick the Great treated intelligence not as support for strategy but as its foundation. His campaigns reveal a timeless truth: a strategist’s advantage begins long before armies meet. First, Frederick built a culture of constant reporting. Scouts, spies, diplomatic envoys, merchants, deserters, and even traveling musicians were folded into a vast informal network. He believed no piece of information was too small; patterns emerge from accumulation, not single revelations. Second, he practiced anticipatory mapping. Frederick kept detailed maps updated at all times, studying rivers, woods, hills, villages, and roads until he could visualize entire regions mentally. This gave him immediate situational awareness—he could calculate routes, ambush points, and deployment lines faster than opponents could even open their map cases. Third, intelligence served preemption. Frederick never waited passively for threats to mature. When Austria prepared to attack, he marched first; when Russia repositioned its armies, he struck before they synchronized. His philosophy was simple: the first mind to understand reality should become the first hand to shape it. Fourth, he weaponized counter-intelligence. Frederick planted false rumors, staged visible troop movements, and manipulated diplomats to distort enemy perception. He understood that misleading an opponent’s decision cycle is as powerful as speeding your own. Fifth, he integrated intelligence into logistics. Frederick adjusted supply depots, marching corridors, and reinforcement paths based on reconnaissance, allowing him to support rapid campaigns without collapse. Sixth, intelligence shaped Frederick’s risk tolerance. Contrary to popular myth, he did not take reckless gambles; he took calculated risks informed by volumes of data. The better he understood the theater, the bolder he could act. Seventh, he believed intelligence must be decentralized. Field commanders were encouraged to gather local information and make adjustments without waiting for royal approval. This prevented paralysis and allowed Prussia to move with the flexibility of a lighter organism. Eighth, Frederick’s system teaches modern strategists that information is not merely a precursor to action; it is a form of power. The side that understands first, decides first. The side that decides first, dictates reality.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
