    -d '{"text": "Frederick the Great engineered one of the most disciplined armies in history—a force that moved, aimed, fired, and reloaded with machine-like precision long before machines existed. Prussian drill was not mere obedience training; it was Frederick’s attempt to solve the fundamental chaos of battle. First, he understood that in real combat, confusion, noise, and terror degrade human performance. Drill was the antidote. He believed that when fear overwhelms conscious thought, the body must fall back on deeply engraved patterns: step, aim, fire, wheel, reform. Second, Frederick reinvented musketry. Prussian soldiers were trained to fire three to five rounds per minute—nearly double the European average. This transformed firepower from episodic volleys into continuous psychological pressure that shattered enemy cohesion. Third, drill enabled maneuver under fire. While most armies became static once firing began, Prussian units could advance, pivot, and change frontage without losing formation integrity. This gave Frederick a mobility advantage even in dense musket engagements. Fourth, he standardized command language. Orders were reduced to short, sharp commands that required no interpretation. Rhythm replaced complexity. Fifth, Frederick believed discipline must be both external and internal. External discipline came from repetition, inspection, and immediate correction. Internal discipline came from pride: belonging to the most precise army in Europe created a sense of elite identity. Sixth, drill allowed combination. Infantry, cavalry, and artillery moved as a single organism. Frederick’s oblique-order attack depended on coordinated timing—something impossible without uniform training. Seventh, he used drill to equalize variables. Different levels of intelligence, courage, or physical strength became less relevant when every soldier was conditioned to act in identical patterns. Precision became the great equalizer. Eighth, Frederick teaches modern strategists that perfect execution is not talent but engineering. You cannot expect clarity under fire unless you build it before fire.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
