    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Frederick the Great understood that cannons do not fire on courage alone—they fire on coin. Prussia’s survival required transforming a fragile agrarian economy into a disciplined war engine without destroying the society that fed it. First, he centralized fiscal control. Frederick demanded that revenue streams—from taxes, domains, monopolies—flow through accountable channels. Leakage was treated as strategic sabotage, not mere corruption. In a small state, every diverted thaler weakened the front line.  Second, he diversified sources of income. Rather than rely purely on land taxes, he encouraged manufactories, trade routes, and controlled state enterprises. Economic variety created resilience. When war disrupted one stream, others could still flow. For Frederick, a balanced ledger was a weapon system.  Third, he practiced frugality at the top. The king lived relatively simply, avoided extravagant courts, and discouraged wasteful luxury among nobles. This was not moralism but math: money not burned on vanity could be turned into muskets, grain, and pay. His austerity set a tone that made sacrifice politically bearable.  Fourth, he linked economic policy to strategic geography. Frederick drained marshes, improved roads, and settled new colonists in sparsely populated areas. Every new village became both a tax base and a logistics node. Territory was not just land to be defended, but soil to be activated.  Fifth, he used credit carefully. Frederick borrowed when necessary but feared the trap of unsustainable debt. War financed purely on future promises can win battles while losing the long peace. He aimed to keep Prussia solvent enough that its signature still meant something.  Sixth, he shielded the productive core where possible. Even in hardship, he tried to protect farmers from total ruin, knowing that scorched peasantry today meant empty granaries tomorrow. Strategic exploitation is different from mindless extraction.  The economic engine of war in Frederick’s hands shows that strategy is not just about maneuvers on the map but flows in the treasury. A commander who ignores the balance sheet fights with borrowed time. A leader who knows how money and power reinforce each other can turn even a poor, landlocked state into a force that great empires are forced to respect.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
