    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Frederick the Great knew that an army marches on paperwork as much as on boots. Behind his campaigns stood a reformed Prussian state that could extract, organize, and apply resources with unusual efficiency. First, he tightened the bureaucracy without suffocating it. Officials were expected to be competent, punctual, and accountable; Frederick read reports, wrote marginal notes, and dismissed those who treated office as sinecure. Administration became a discipline, not a social prize.  Second, he encouraged merit over pure birth, at least within limits. While Prussia remained aristocratic, Frederick opened paths for talented commoners in the civil service and army. Ability became a partial currency alongside lineage. The state’s machine functioned better when staffed by people chosen for skill rather than salon brilliance.  Third, he simplified legal and administrative procedures. Frederick’s reforms aimed to make justice faster and more predictable, contracts clearer, and property rights more secure. Predictability is a strategic asset: citizens who understand the rules can plan, invest, and accept sacrifice for the state with less resentment.  Fourth, he visited provinces, inspected courts, and listened to grievances. This was not mere theater; it created a feedback loop. A distant state soon becomes clumsy; a state that occasionally looks at itself can still adjust. Frederick’s presence in the provinces signaled that competence mattered everywhere, not just in Berlin.  Fifth, he integrated military and civil administration. War was not something that happened far away while the state remained unchanged at home. Taxation, conscription, infrastructure, and legal norms all bent slightly around the needs of defense. The Prussian state was not a neutral shell; it was a tailored instrument.  For modern strategists, Frederick’s reforms show that power is not just the size of an army, but the quality of the system that feeds it. A cluttered bureaucracy, slow courts, and opaque rules are forms of national self-sabotage. The most brilliant strategic thinking fails if the institutions beneath it cannot carry decisions into daily reality. Frederick understood that building a state was itself a strategic act.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
