    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Prussia under Frederick the Great was not a rich empire; it was a narrow, vulnerable state with limited population, territory, and money. Yet Frederick learned to turn scarcity into sharpness. First, he prioritized ruthlessly. Not every frontier could be equally defended, not every project equally funded. He concentrated investment where it yielded disproportionate strategic effects: the army, key fortresses, critical roads, and select administrative reforms. Prestige projects with low utility found little sympathy.  Second, he accepted trade-offs openly. Committing to one theater meant neglecting another; reinforcing one ally meant leaving another to fend for itself. Frederick did not pretend that choices were costless. By facing the pain of each decision consciously, he avoided the illusion that he could do everything.  Third, he treated manpower as precious. Losses that larger states could absorb might be fatal for Prussia. This did not make him timid—his campaigns were often daring—but it made him sensitive to attrition. He sought decisive operations that destroyed enemy cohesion rather than endless grinding battles of consumption.  Fourth, he used diplomacy as a force multiplier. When resources are limited, you must borrow power from the choices of others. Frederick’s alliances, truces, and propaganda aimed to shape the behavior of larger states so they unintentionally supported his survival by fighting each other, hesitating, or withdrawing.  Fifth, he invested heavily in quality. If you cannot match numbers, you must exceed in training, drill, and conceptual clarity. Prussian infantry under Frederick was feared not because of sheer size but because of its performance per soldier. Excellence per unit became a strategic doctrine.  For modern readers, Frederick’s use of limited resources illustrates that strategy begins with honest accounting. Nations, organizations, and individuals who misjudge their capacity design fantasies rather than plans. Scarcity, when acknowledged, can focus effort, sharpen thinking, and force creative combinations. It is not an excuse to do nothing; it is a reason to design better.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/frederick_v2_7_en.mp3"
