    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "By the late years of his reign, Napoleon faced not only external enemies but an internal adversary more formidable: exhaustion. Human will, even at its most titanic, has limits. Volume III demands we study the psychological dimension of collapse. First, Napoleon lost the freshness of perception. Early in his career he saw patterns no one else saw; later, he saw only reflections of past victories. His mind, once fluid, became algorithmic—predictable, rigid, less curious. Second, exhaustion eroded emotional discipline. Reports from 1812–1814 describe impatience, bursts of anger, long silences, and an inability to listen. These cracks—small on the surface—altered strategic judgment profoundly. Third, his motivational reservoir dried. Napoleon no longer fought for a world he hoped to build, but for a world he feared to lose. Defensive willpower lacks the creative spark that fuels great strategy. Fourth, exhaustion distorted time perception. He rushed decisions, compressed planning cycles, and misread pacing. Leipzig, the 1814 campaign, Waterloo—all reveal a man fighting against time rather than shaping it. Fifth, exhaustion weakened his relational intelligence. He misjudged allies, underestimated opponents, overestimated loyalty, and dismissed warnings. The man who once read human nature with razor clarity now saw in blur. Sixth, he lost the capacity to regenerate. In earlier decades, rest, reflection, or a single campaign could restore him. But empire-scale stress accumulates faster than recovery. Seventh, exhaustion amplified strategic illusions: the belief that one more victory would reverse systemic decline, that Europe feared him more than it did, that morale could be willed into existence. Eighth, inner collapse is quiet. Napoleon did not break visibly; he dwindled. A strategist must recognize that willpower is a finite resource—and that sustaining inner health is part of statecraft. Exhaustion is not weakness; it is a physics of the mind. When the internal engine cracks, even the greatest leader becomes a shadow of his earlier self.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
