    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Hannibal mastered the art of chaos control: turning disorder into a weapon. Unlike Rome, whose strength lay in discipline and predictability, Hannibal thrived in fluid and uncertain environments. He believed that a smaller force must embrace complexity to overwhelm a structured opponent. Chaos, when shaped deliberately, becomes a strategic tool. Hannibal used mixed armies—Numidian cavalry, Iberian infantry, Gauls, Libyans—each with unique fighting styles. What seemed like fragmentation was, in his hands, mosaic cohesion. He exploited diversity to create unpredictability, confusing Roman commanders who preferred uniform formations and fixed doctrine. Chaos control also appeared in his operational tempo. Hannibal moved rapidly, shifted theaters unexpectedly, and manipulated terrain to force Rome into reactive positions. His ambush at Lake Trasimene exemplifies this mastery: he used fog, terrain, psychological deception, and split-force movements to envelope a Roman army without giving them a chance to form ranks. Chaos was not an accident; it was engineered. Modern strategists can learn from this approach: when outmatched, do not fight on the opponent’s terms. Instead, generate conditions where adaptation becomes the decisive factor. Hannibal shows that the commander who choreographs chaos can override numerical inferiority, bend the battlefield to his will, and fracture the mental stability of a stronger foe.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/hannibal_v1_2_en.mp3"
