    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Hannibal’s army was not a unified nation but a coalition of tribes, mercenaries, and allies bound together by respect, fear, opportunity, and shared hardship. Managing this mosaic of interests required diplomatic genius equal to his military talent. Unlike Rome, whose legions were culturally integrated and state-funded, Hannibal commanded warriors with differing loyalties, customs, and motivations. His coalition strategy worked because he understood the psychology of groups. He rewarded bravery publicly, punished betrayal decisively, and maintained strict fairness among ethnic factions. Hannibal also used culture as a diplomatic weapon. He adopted local customs when operating in Gaul, distributed war spoils equitably, and allowed tribal leaders symbolic autonomy. This approach created loyalty based on dignity rather than coercion. Rivals within the coalition were neutralized through personal negotiation or by assigning them roles that aligned their self-interest with his strategic goals. Modern parallels include multinational coalitions, corporate alliances, and cross-cultural team leadership. Hannibal teaches that diverse groups cannot be unified by force alone; they require relational intelligence, equitable treatment, and shared mission. His coalition endured years of hardship in foreign lands—proof that inclusive leadership can bind even fragmented forces into a coherent strategic entity.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
