    -d '{"text": "Hannibal’s campaign in Italy is often remembered for its opening brilliance—Trebia, Trasimene, Cannae—but its deeper lesson lies in what followed: a long war of exhaustion that he could not win. After the shock of his victories, Rome refused to negotiate. Instead, it adapted. Hannibal found himself in a hostile land, far from home, with no secure port, no direct supply line, and no simple path to a decisive political settlement. First, he tried to turn the war into a political landslide. Hannibal marched through Italy hoping that Rome’s allies would fall away. Some did; many did not. The Roman alliance proved more resilient than his expectation. Local grievances were real, but fear of Rome, habit, and lack of trust in Carthage limited defections. Hannibal’s tactical victories did not automatically convert into a new political map. Second, the economics of a long campaign turned against him. Rome could draw on a vast manpower pool, re-levy legions, and shift the war to Spain, Sicily, and the seas. Hannibal, cut off from Carthage for long stretches, had to live off the land and whatever aid could be smuggled through. Each year that passed eroded his relative strength. Third, the psychological arc of the war changed. At first, he embodied terror; Roman armies marched in fear of another Cannae. But as time passed and Rome avoided pitched battles, the legend of Hannibal shifted from immediate threat to lingering burden. Fear hardened into grim determination. Hannibal remained dangerous, but no longer destiny. Fourth, he confronted the limits of operating alone. Hannibal was the spearhead of a strategy that Carthage never fully matched with resources or a coherent grand plan. Without synchronized offensives elsewhere, his presence in Italy became a bleeding point rather than a true center of gravity. The long war of exhaustion teaches that even the most dazzling opening cannot sustain itself without reinforcement from politics, economics, and allies. Strategy is not just how you strike, but how you go on living with the consequences of the blow. Hannibal’s genius built an opening his system could not support.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
