    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Caesar’s campaigns in Gaul reveal one of his greatest strategic advantages: intelligence warfare. Long before the modern era, Caesar developed a sophisticated information system that allowed him to predict enemy movements, anticipate rebellions, understand terrain intricacies, and manipulate tribal politics. He recognized that Gaul was not a unified enemy but a mosaic of tribes with shifting loyalties, rivalries, and ambitions. Intelligence gathering allowed him to transform this complexity into a battlefield advantage. Caesar recruited local scouts, defectors, merchants, interpreters, and even former enemies to build an extensive human intelligence network. Every tribe had informants willing to trade information for protection or favor. Caesar rewarded those who delivered accurate intelligence and punished deception severely, creating strong incentives for reliability. He also mastered the use of reconnaissance. Roman cavalry and light infantry probed enemy positions, mapped forests, rivers, choke points, and discovered supply lines. Caesar often concealed his true plans until the last moment, denying enemies a clear picture of Roman intentions. Perhaps his greatest intelligence skill was political reading. He understood which tribes were likely to rebel, which leaders were ambitious, which coalitions were unstable, and which rivalries he could inflame. His ability to strike preemptively—before rebellions grew—came from this insight. Intelligence also served psychological warfare. Caesar leaked information selectively, spread rumors to weaken enemy morale, and manipulated perceptions of Roman power. The Commentaries themselves functioned as strategic messaging, shaping how Rome interpreted events and ensuring political support for his campaigns. The deeper lesson is that Caesar viewed information not as an accessory to war but as the foundation of decision-making. He fought with knowledge first and steel second. In Gaul, battles were often won before the first spear was thrown, because Caesar already knew how the enemy would react. Intelligence warfare allowed him to dominate a continent not just by force but by foresight.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
