    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Crossing the Rubicon is one of history’s most iconic strategic decisions. When Caesar stepped across that small river with a single legion, he ignited a civil war that would end the Roman Republic and reshape the political architecture of the Western world. The brilliance of the decision lies in its mixture of inevitability, timing, psychological boldness, and narrative mastery. Caesar understood that returning to Rome without troops meant imprisonment or assassination. His enemies controlled the Senate, manipulated legal procedures, and framed him as a threat to the Republic. Caesar faced a paradox: obeying the law meant destruction; breaking it meant war. His genius was recognizing that neutrality was impossible. By acting decisively, he seized initiative and framed the conflict as self-defense. Timing was essential. He waited until Pompey and the Senate had overreached, issuing orders that appeared unjust and fear-driven. Caesar knew the public resented corruption and elite manipulation. When he crossed the Rubicon, he presented his move as liberation, not rebellion. Psychological dominance shaped the moment. Caesar’s words, reportedly “Let the die be cast,” conveyed acceptance of destiny and absolute resolve. This unsettled his enemies, who expected hesitation. Instead, Caesar moved with stunning speed, capturing cities without bloodshed and turning elite defections into political victories. His greatest strategic weapon at this stage was narrative. He insisted he sought peace, blamed the Senate for escalation, and portrayed Pompey as abandoning Rome. This framing shaped the morale of both sides. Crossing the Rubicon teaches that strategy sometimes requires embracing irreversible action when every alternative leads to loss. It also shows the power of timing, narrative, and decisive will. Caesar won not because he had more troops—he had fewer—but because he controlled the story, tempo, and psychological terrain. The moment he crossed the Rubicon, Rome’s future shifted from senatorial oligarchy to imperial destiny.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
