    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Caesar’s cultural identity strategy was a quiet revolution that changed Rome from a conqueror of peoples into a creator of civilizations. He understood that political power becomes fragile when it rests only on force. Lasting empires shape identity, not just territory. Caesar’s approach began with a simple insight: people accept rule more easily when they can see themselves reflected in the ruling power. Therefore, his strategy fused Roman authority with local pride, allowing conquered peoples to feel elevated rather than erased. The first element was symbolic inclusion. Caesar integrated provincial elites into Roman rituals, ceremonies, and public honors. Wearing Roman garments, attending Roman festivals, or receiving Roman titles allowed local leaders to feel part of a prestigious world order. Inclusion created loyalty far deeper than coercion. The second element was controlled Romanization. Unlike later emperors, Caesar did not force cultural assimilation. He allowed languages, religions, and customs to persist. Roman law and administration were introduced gradually. This created an ecosystem where Roman structures guided life without overwhelming identity. The third element was mythmaking. Caesar used literature and public narrative to portray Rome as the guardian of civilization. In this narrative, conquered peoples did not lose—they joined. Caesar himself became a semi-mythic figure, bridging local worlds through charisma and symbolism. The fourth element was architectural imprint. Caesar built forums, temples, and civic spaces across territories, blending Roman design with local aesthetics. Architecture is the most durable propaganda; it turns political narratives into stone. The fifth element was generational strategy. Caesar understood identity transforms slowly. His policies aimed at long-term change through education, intermarriage, military service, and shared civic life. Over time, these created hybrid identities that were culturally rich but politically Roman. The lesson is profound: empires collapse when they demand sameness but thrive when they create belonging. Caesar built identity not through erasure but expansion, persuading diverse peoples to imagine Rome as part of themselves. This psychological foundation would support the empire long after Caesar was gone.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/caesar_v2_5_en.mp3"
