    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Reinvention was one of Caesar’s most profound strategic tools. He understood that leaders who remain fixed in a single identity become predictable, and predictable leaders become vulnerable. Caesar therefore cultivated the ability to transform himself repeatedly, adjusting his image, alliances, and roles to match the evolving needs of Rome and his own ambitions. At each stage of his ascent, Caesar reshaped how both allies and enemies perceived him. In his early years, he played the part of the refined nobleman, charming the elite and mastering rhetoric. During his military rise, he became the warrior statesman, building a loyal army through charisma, shared hardship, and a promise of glory. While in Gaul, he adopted the role of liberator and civilizer, framing his brutal conquests as acts of protection. Upon returning to Rome, he shifted again, portraying himself as a stabilizing force capable of ending senatorial chaos. Reinvention allowed Caesar to remain one step ahead of political traps. He understood that power is a fluid environment; leaders survive by shifting shape, not by standing still. His transformations were never superficial. They were strategic recalibrations designed to preserve momentum. Caesar’s reinvention also extended to institutions. He reshaped Rome’s calendar, reformed debt, expanded citizenship, and reorganized provincial governance. These actions presented him as a modernizing visionary rather than a tyrant. The Senate feared his ambition, but the people saw his innovations as progress. Reinvention also insulated Caesar against backlash. When accused of accumulating too much power, he emphasized clemency. When accused of being too lenient, he demonstrated decisive force. He adjusted posture without losing core direction. The lesson is clear: in politics, identity is a strategic asset that must evolve. Caesar’s greatness came not only from brilliance in battle but from an extraordinary ability to become whatever the moment required. Reinvention is not betrayal of the self; it is mastery of circumstance. Leaders who cannot transform eventually become relics. Caesar’s legacy endures because he understood that greatness is not a fixed state but a continual act of becoming.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
