    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Musashi places enormous emphasis on the strategic control of distance—maai. He states that victory is often decided before swords clash, in the subtle psychological and spatial contest of who controls the zone of engagement. Distance is not merely physical; it is also mental, emotional, and strategic. To Musashi, controlling distance means dictating the terms on which conflict occurs. He identifies three distances: far distance, where observation dominates; middle distance, where deception and positioning matter; and close distance, where decisive action occurs. A strategist must transition between these ranges without hesitation. Many lose because their minds cling to one distance; they cannot adapt when the opponent shifts unexpectedly. Musashi explains that controlling distance grants several powers: the power to observe without being observed, the power to threaten without committing, the power to force hesitation in the opponent, and the power to decide when engagement becomes inevitable. Distance becomes a lever that bends the opponent’s will. Whoever manages distance manages tempo; whoever manages tempo manages victory. He teaches that distance is also psychological. When you are too close emotionally—angry, fearful, or attached—you cannot see clearly. When you are too distant—detached to the point of apathy—you lose urgency. The strategist must maintain the perfect internal maai—close enough to act decisively, distant enough to remain objective. Musashi also shows how distance interacts with rhythm. Expanding and contracting distance shapes tempo, forcing the opponent into rhythms they cannot control. In negotiation or leadership, this appears as strategic pacing: stepping back to drain pressure, stepping forward to impose momentum. Modern applications include military movement, market positioning, competitive strategy, interpersonal conflict, leadership presence, and psychological boundaries. A leader who controls emotional and strategic distance does not get pulled into reactivity. Musashi concludes that distance is both a weapon and a shield. The one who manages space—internal and external—governs the entire battlefield, long before the first strike is made.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
