    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Musashi believed the highest form of victory is breaking the enemy’s spirit before breaking their body. He writes that opponents collapse internally long before the decisive strike. To Musashi, psychological defeat is strategic inevitability—the body merely follows the mind. He explains that breaking spirit requires understanding human fear, hesitation, ego, pride, and the instinct for self-preservation. A strategist who understands these forces can shape the battlefield of the opponent’s mind. Musashi used several methods. First is **imposing inevitability**, projecting total confidence so the opponent senses they cannot win. Second is **stealing rhythm**, disrupting timing so the opponent cannot execute familiar patterns. Third is **overwhelming presence**, filling the space so completely that the opponent feels suffocated. Fourth is **attacking intention**, striking before the opponent’s decision fully forms. He stresses that the strategist must maintain an unbroken spirit themselves. If one’s spirit cracks—even momentarily—the opponent senses it. Musashi wrote that spirit transmits like scent. Strong spirit elevates allies and unravels enemies. Musashi also taught the art of inducing emotional imbalance. Anger blinds; arrogance opens gaps; fear slows movement; over-eagerness exposes weaknesses. By manipulating emotion—through silence, gaze, pace, or disruption—the strategist guides the opponent into self-destruction. He warns that breaking spirit must never be cruel for its own sake. It is a strategic act, not an emotional one. Emotional cruelty blinds the strategist, while calm domination sharpens them. Modern relevance includes crisis leadership, competitive pressure, courtroom strategy, political debate, market disruption, negotiation, and high-stakes conflict. In these arenas, parties often collapse not because of facts, but because their will erodes. Musashi’s lesson: the true battlefield is the mind. When the enemy’s spirit breaks, resistance becomes symbolic. Defeat becomes a matter of formality. Victory belongs not to the strongest body, but the strongest spirit.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
