    -d '{"text": "While Saladin is mostly remembered for inland battles, his strategic success depended heavily on sea and supply. First, he understood that Crusader states were maritime organisms. Their lifelines ran through ports like Tyre, Acre, and Jaffa. Controlling or threatening these nodes meant influencing the flow of European men and money. Second, he invested in shipbuilding and alliances with coastal cities, even if Muslim fleets could not fully match Italian or Frankish navies. Third, he used naval pressure to complement land sieges, making it harder for besieged fortresses to receive aid. Fourth, he built inland logistical corridors: caravan routes, depots, and fortified waystations that ensured his own armies could move and be supplied without depending on fragile coastal lines. Fifth, he aligned logistics with climate. Campaigns were timed to avoid the worst summers when water was scarce, or winters when roads became impassable. Sixth, he diversified supply sources. Grain from Egypt, levies from Syria, and horses from the steppe hinterlands created redundancy. Seventh, he protected pilgrims and merchants when possible, knowing that economic vitality fed his war machine. Eighth, he shows that strategy is not just about where armies meet, but about the quiet architecture of ports, roads, and warehouses that make any meeting possible in the first place.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
