    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Saladin’s military fame can obscure his work as a governor. Yet his ability to fund campaigns, keep populations mostly supportive, and prevent catastrophic uprisings depended on how he ruled Egypt and Syria. First, he sought to be perceived as just. Chronicles emphasize his accessibility to petitioners, willingness to overturn unjust rulings, and efforts to curb the worst abuses of tax collectors. Justice here was not abstract; it was a tool to keep the governed invested in the new order. Second, he rationalized taxation. Rather than looting arbitrarily, he moved toward more regularized levies and tried to protect key productive sectors like agriculture and trade. War needs revenue, but excessive extraction kills the host. Third, he staffed administration with a mix of loyal Kurds, Turks, and established Egyptian and Syrian bureaucrats, blending continuity with change. This reduced the shock of regime transition. Fourth, he used public works—canals, fortifications, religious buildings—to signal that the state gave as well as took. Fifth, he practiced selective severity. Banditry, rebellion, or open corruption were punished sharply, sending clear signals about which norms mattered. Sixth, he respected local customs where possible, avoiding unnecessary cultural disruption. For modern strategists, Saladin’s governance reminds us that military operations sit on a foundation of administration. You cannot sustain long campaigns, intelligence networks, or alliances if your tax base is collapsing, your officials are hated, and your justice is despised. The art is to convert the coercive power that wins battles into institutional power that people can live with. Saladin did not create a perfect state, but he built one resilient enough to support a generational struggle.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
