    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "The encounters between Saladin and Richard the Lionheart during the Third Crusade have become legendary. For strategy, they illustrate a duel of prestige under the shadow of limited war. Both men needed to appear valiant defenders of faith and honor, yet neither could afford total ruin. Saladin respected Richard as a formidable battlefield commander. He avoided offering a single pitched battle in conditions favorable to the English king, preferring harassment, maneuver, and negotiation. Richard, in turn, respected Saladin’s control of depth and supplies, recognizing that even if he won tactical victories, pushing too far inland could overextend him. Their exchanges of gifts, physicians, and courtesies were not mere romance; they were instruments of signaling. Each side reassured the other that, within ferocious conflict, certain boundaries would be maintained. Neither side, for example, systematically massacred noncombatants on the scale seen in earlier wars. Both were playing to multiple audiences: their own followers, foreign courts, and God. The eventual compromise—Muslim control of Jerusalem with Christian access rights—reflected this logic of limited objectives under high symbolic stakes. For modern strategists, the Saladin–Richard duel suggests that even arch-enemies can recognize mutual constraints. When both sides understand that annihilation would trigger wider catastrophe, warfare becomes a negotiation with violence rather than a pure contest of extinction. The art is to fight hard enough to defend vital interests, yet remain open to terms that preserve the possibility of future coexistence.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
