    -d '{"text": "Scipio Africanus’ African campaign was the proving ground where he evolved beyond traditional Roman command and demonstrated a style that blended diplomacy, speed, and cultural intelligence. Upon arriving in Iberia, he inherited a fractured theater: hostile Carthaginian commanders, hesitant tribes, and Roman legions still traumatized by Hannibal’s victories. His first insight was that Iberia could not be won purely through force; it required legitimacy. He studied local customs, honored tribal leaders, respected regional sanctuaries, and insisted Roman troops maintain discipline. This moral posture built trust. Scipio’s second insight was the power of targeted shock. Instead of spreading his forces thin, he struck Carthaginian strongpoints sequentially, beginning with New Carthage. His lightning assault—using coordinated infantry, naval landings, and a surprise wading maneuver through shallow waters—shattered the Carthaginian perception of Roman rigidity. The rapid fall of the city not only yielded prisoners, treasuries, and hostages but also won admiration from Iberian tribes, who interpreted the victory as a sign of divine favor. His third insight was the importance of economic liberation. Scipio freed hostages taken by Carthage, reduced tax burdens, and punished corrupt intermediaries. This policy turned Iberian resentment against Carthaginian rule into loyalty toward Rome. Fourth: Scipio used alliances as multipliers. By treating Iberian leaders as partners rather than subjects, he gained auxiliary forces with terrain knowledge and cavalry unmatched by Roman units. Fifth: adaptive maneuvering. Scipio observed that Iberian terrain favored fluidity. He designed looser formations, encouraged skirmishing, and used flexible detachments rather than rigid manipular blocks. This allowed him to outmaneuver Carthaginian forces accustomed to predictable Roman patterns. Sixth: psychological restoration. Scipio’s victories restored Roman confidence abroad a