    -d '{"text": "Scipio Africanus mastered alliance-building not as negotiation but as engineering—constructing networks of interdependence that made loyalty rational for partners. His approach contrasted sharply with Carthaginian practice, which often relied on coercion or transactional loyalty. Scipio believed alliances must bind through dignity and shared advantage. First: recognize local power structures. Upon entering Iberia, Scipio studied tribal rivalries, chieftain lineages, and historic feuds. He understood that alliances are not made with abstract populations but with individuals whose pride, memory, and political stakes shape decisions. Second: elevate allies rather than absorb them. Scipio treated Iberian and Numidian leaders as partners, not vassals. He respected their ceremonies, consulted them in councils, and rewarded victories publicly. This gave allies status, which Carthaginian commanders often denied. Third: moral reciprocity. Scipio returned hostages, protected villages, and enforced fair treatment. By acting predictably and ethically, he made Roman partnership safer than Carthaginian rule. Fourth: targeted incentives. Scipio did not simply bribe leaders; he offered long-term gains—access to Roman markets, military protection, and political recognition. Fifth: divide-and-cohere strategy. Rather than indiscriminately uniting everyone, Scipio allied selectively, choosing leaders who could shift regional dynamics. By strengthening Masinissa over Syphax, he altered North Africa’s power balance permanently. Sixth: relational intelligence. Scipio built personal rapport through conversation, shared rituals, and displays of honor. Leaders followed him because they liked him, not only because they feared or needed Rome. Seventh: alliance discipline. Betrayal had consequences, but Scipio avoided cruelty. Punishment was proportional, preserving dignity and preventing cycles of vengeance. Eighth: logistical cooperation. Allies were integrated into supply l