    -d '{"text": "Scipio Africanus never tried to be Hannibal. Instead, he tried to outlearn Hannibal. This distinction is critical for modern strategists who face dominant competitors or iconic leaders. First, he studied the Cannae disaster not as humiliation but as curriculum. The annihilation of Roman legions became a textbook for what not to do—rigid formations, prideful aggression, underestimation of cavalry, and blindness to encirclement risks. Second, Scipio built a counter-method instead of a counter-move. Rather than chase specific tricks, he asked: what is the underlying grammar of Hannibal’s art? Fluidity of formation, exploitation of morale, use of terrain, tempo control. Then he designed Roman reforms—more flexible maniples, deeper reserves, and adaptive deployment—to systematically blunt that grammar. Third, he upgraded intelligence. Scipio emphasized reconnaissance, local informants, and rapid feedback from skirmishes. Knowing how Hannibal thought was as important as knowing where he was. Fourth, he trained officers to think, not just obey. While Hannibal’s army relied heavily on his personal genius, Scipio groomed a cadre capable of independent adjustment within his intent. This created a distributed intelligence that could respond even when he was absent. Fifth, Scipio learned psychologically. He understood that Hannibal’s legend itself was a weapon. By winning clear, symbolic victories in Iberia and Africa, he eroded that myth in Roman minds and among allies. The aura of invincibility cracked. Outlearning a genius opponent means accepting their superiority in some dimensions while building a system that makes that superiority less relevant. Scipio teaches that strategy is not about being the brightest mind in the room; it is about building the smartest learning architecture around you.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
