    -d '{"text": "Your mind lives in maps, not in reality. A “map” is your model of how things work; the “territory” is the world as it actually is. Strategy collapses when you defend the map instead of updating it. To practice this model, separate observation from interpretation. Observation is what you can record with a camera; interpretation is the story you add. For example, “sales dropped 8%” is observation; “customers hate us” is interpretation. Treat interpretations as hypotheses, not facts. Ask: what else could be true? What data would disconfirm my story? The map-territory gap grows under stress because the brain simplifies, tribalizes, and seeks certainty. That is why executives must develop “epistemic humility”: the confidence to act, paired with the willingness to revise. A useful ritual is a weekly “map audit.” Choose one belief that guided your week—about a person, a market, your health, your productivity—and test it against results. If the world contradicted you, update the model quickly and visibly. Another practice is “steel manning”: articulate the strongest version of the opposing view before rejecting it. This forces your map to include territory you might avoid. Finally, build maps at the right resolution. Too detailed and you freeze; too vague and you hallucinate. Good maps are simple, predictive, and revisable. The strategist is not the person with the loudest certainty. The strategist is the person whose maps keep getting closer to the territory, so their moves keep working.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
