    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Thucydides observed that the deepest cause of the Peloponnesian War was the rise of Athens and the fear this inspired in Sparta. This dynamic, repeated throughout history, creates a structural trap: when a rising power grows rapidly in wealth, influence, and ambition, the established power feels its long-standing dominance threatened. Even without aggression, the emerging power’s very success appears dangerous; its allies become emboldened, its political model gains prestige, and its capabilities expand. Meanwhile, the ruling power interprets these developments through the lens of loss—loss of status, loss of security, loss of control. Such fear drives defensive measures that feel offensive to the rising power, which then reacts with hostility, creating a feedback loop of mistrust. Thucydides teaches that power transitions are rarely peaceful because both sides misinterpret the other’s intentions. The rising power believes it deserves greater influence; the ruling power believes it is defending stability. Each expects the other to back down. Neither adjusts quickly enough to avoid collision. The tragedy is not just that conflict becomes likely but that both sides believe they are acting preventively, even virtuously. For modern strategists, the Thucydidean Trap warns against assuming that goodwill, treaties, or interdependence eliminate structural tension. States compete because they inhabit a system where relative power matters more than absolute comfort. To escape the trap, leaders must redesign perceptions, manage fear, and restructure incentives so that adjustment replaces confrontation. Thucydides does not claim war is inevitable—but he reveals why it becomes the logical outcome when states fail to understand the psychological architecture of power transitions.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v1_2_en.mp3"
