fi

# [1190/1298] thucydides_v2_1_en.mp3
if [ ! -f "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v2_1_en.mp3" ]; then
  echo "[1190/1298] Generating: thucydides_v2_1_en.mp3"
  curl -s -X POST \
    "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech/${VOICE_ID}" \
    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Thucydides framed the Peloponnesian War around a simple but devastating insight: the rise of Athens and the fear it inspired in Sparta made war inevitable. This logic of power transitions remains one of the most important strategic principles in history. First, rising powers generate fear because they alter expectations about future capability. Even peaceful growth becomes threatening, because others imagine what that power might do later. Second, established powers face psychological erosion. Their identity, status, and historical role are challenged, creating anxiety that distorts judgment. Fear becomes a lens that magnifies ambiguous actions into threats. Third, rising powers misread their own ascent. Confidence transforms into entitlement; they see resistance as irrational obstruction rather than legitimate defensive behavior. Fourth, both sides experience narrative drift. Each constructs a story in which it is the victim reacting to the provocations of the other. Thucydides shows how mutual insecurity hardens into mutual certainty. Fifth, leaders manipulate transition dynamics. Athenian politicians invoked honor and destiny to justify expansion, while Spartan elites invoked fear and ancestral duty to justify preemptive war. Sixth, domestic factions weaponize the shift. Hawks exploit fear to gain influence; moderates are accused of weakness; internal politics accelerates external escalation. Seventh, the security dilemma becomes self-fulfilling. Defensive moves look offensive, causing spirals of misinterpretation. Eighth, miscalculation grows unavoidable. Each side expects the other to back down; each side expects a short conflict; each underestimates the resilience of the other’s alliances. Thucydides teaches that power transitions are dangerous not because of arithmetic strength but because of the psychology and narratives they unleash. Modern rivalries echo this pattern: rising powers believe they deserve more space; established powers believe they must defend the order they built. The tragic insight of Thucydides is that both sides often act rationally within their own worldview and still create a collision course. Avoiding this tragedy requires acknowledging the emotional and symbolic dimensions of power transitions—not only the material ones.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v2_1_en.mp3"
