fi

# [1191/1298] thucydides_v2_3_en.mp3
if [ ! -f "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v2_3_en.mp3" ]; then
  echo "[1191/1298] Generating: thucydides_v2_3_en.mp3"
  curl -s -X POST \
    "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech/${VOICE_ID}" \
    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "The Sicilian Expedition is one of history’s purest examples of strategic hubris: a democracy seduced by its own legend, a leadership elite blinded by ambition, and an empire stretched beyond the limits of wisdom. For Thucydides, Sicily was not a mistake of planning but a mistake of character. First, Athens believed that past success guaranteed future triumphs. The victories of the early war had built a myth of Athenian inevitability; caution became unfashionable. Second, leaders conflated ambition with destiny. Alcibiades argued that expansion was Athens’ natural right, a matter of honor and identity. Opponents were framed as cowards lacking imagination, making dissent politically dangerous. Third, the city underestimated the cost of projecting power across distance. It imagined Sicily as a quick strike rather than a massive logistical challenge. Fourth, intelligence failures compounded arrogance. Reports about Sicilian politics were vague, overly optimistic, or shaped to fit the narrative desired by the Assembly. Fifth, Athens ignored opportunity cost. While chasing glory abroad, it weakened its ability to defend the homeland and exposed itself to Spartan counteraction—exactly what happened when Sparta fortified Decelea. Sixth, the empire misjudged allies. It assumed subject states would continue contributing ships and silver without hesitation, not realizing that overextension eroded loyalty. Seventh, escalation was driven by sunk costs. After early setbacks, instead of withdrawing, Athens doubled down, sending its second fleet—and its last strategic reserve—into a deteriorating situation. Eighth, the enemy adapted faster. The Syracusans, with Spartan and Corinthian help, learned Athenian tactics, fortified their harbors, and transformed initial disadvantages into decisive strength. Ninth, Athens confused boldness with recklessness. It believed greatness required constant expansion, forgetting that restraint is also a form of strategy. The end was catastrophic: fleets annihilated, armies captured, Athens psychologically shattered. Thucydides presents Sicily as a universal warning: empires fall not when they are weak but when they believe they cannot fall. Modern states repeat this pattern when they attempt simultaneous wars, underestimate local resistance, or mistake technological superiority for inevitability. Overreach begins in the mind, long before it appears on the battlefield.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v2_3_en.mp3"
  sleep $DELAY
else
fi

# [1192/1298] thucydides_v2_5_en.mp3
if [ ! -f "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v2_5_en.mp3" ]; then
  echo "[1192/1298] Generating: thucydides_v2_5_en.mp3"
  curl -s -X POST \
    "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech/${VOICE_ID}" \
    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Fear is one of the central currencies of Thucydidean strategy. It shapes alliances, drives escalation, and transforms ordinary disputes into existential conflicts. Thucydides shows that fear is not simply an emotion but a strategic force with its own economy. First, fear spreads faster than information. Rumors, partial reports, or symbolic gestures can generate panic long before facts are clear. This gives leaders an incentive to act preemptively, often violently. Second, fear incentivizes misinterpretation. Actions meant to reassure may instead appear suspicious or aggressive. When states expect hostility, they see hostility everywhere. Third, fear distorts proportionality. Minor threats receive maximum responses. Modest provocations evoke overwhelming retaliation. Fourth, fear rewards extreme voices. Hawks gain influence by promising strength; moderates lose credibility because caution feels dangerous. Fifth, fear corrodes alliances. Allies fear abandonment; powerful partners fear entrapment. Thucydides shows how both Athens and Sparta doubted their own coalition networks even when they were strong on paper. Sixth, fear produces moral inversion. At Corcyra, citizens committed atrocities not out of hatred but because they feared what the other faction would do if it gained power. Seventh, fear creates windows for opportunism. Leaders manipulate it to expand authority, suppress rivals, or justify policies that would normally face resistance. Eighth, fear accelerates time. States feel compelled to act before they are ready. Decisions that should take months are made in hours. Ninth, fear becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy. Expecting treachery, states behave treacherously; expecting aggression, they act aggressively. Thucydides teaches that the management of fear is one of the highest strategic arts. Successful leaders must regulate the emotional climate of their societies, maintain calm communications channels, and prevent panic-driven decisions. Modern parallels are obvious: nuclear alerts, cyber incidents, economic shocks, and crises amplified by media ecosystems. Fear remains a strategic accelerant that can ignite war even when no one desires it.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v2_5_en.mp3"
  sleep $DELAY
else
fi

# [1193/1298] thucydides_v3_1_en.mp3
if [ ! -f "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v3_1_en.mp3" ]; then
  echo "[1193/1298] Generating: thucydides_v3_1_en.mp3"
  curl -s -X POST \
    "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech/${VOICE_ID}" \
    -H "Accept: audio/mpeg" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -H "xi-api-key: ${API_KEY}" \
    -d '{"text": "Thucydides teaches that the deepest tragedies in strategy arise not from evil intentions but from the widening gap between what leaders intend and what their actions actually produce. Athens did not seek self-destruction; Sparta did not seek endless war. Yet both became prisoners of consequences they set in motion. First, intentions are narrow; outcomes are vast. Leaders speak in goals—security, honor, advantage—while the system responds with cascading reactions beyond their control. Second, action creates momentum. A policy meant as a warning becomes a commitment; a deterrent edit becomes a provocation; a symbolic act becomes an irreversible step. Third, fear, honor, and interest reshape decisions as they unfold. Leaders lose sight of original aims and become consumed by pride, revenge, or desperation. Fourth, success produces blindness. Athens misread early victories as proof of invincibility and changed its objectives mid-war without noticing the transformation. Fifth, allies distort plans. Smaller states resist, misbehave, or drag great powers into conflicts not aligned with the original strategy. Sixth, the public creates pressure. Leaders escalate to maintain legitimacy or avoid blame, even when escalation contradicts logic. Seventh, rhetoric replaces clarity. Policies become wrapped in moral language that hides strategic misalignment until too late. Eighth, the system itself evolves. What was once a limited contest becomes total struggle. Thucydides shows that tragedy emerges when leaders cannot withdraw without humiliation, cannot advance without ruin, and cannot stand still without decay. Modern great powers face the same trap: wars begun for narrow aims transform into quagmires; rivals misread signals; commitments multiply; and leaders become trapped by their own narratives. Strategic tragedy is not fate—it is the natural result of ignoring the expanding gap between intention and outcome.", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.85, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}' \
    -o "$AUDIO_DIR/thucydides_v3_1_en.mp3"
